description:
As Paralegal Officer, you will provide generalist legal support to the Legal Team on matters concerning Meliore’s activities globally. Your primary purpose will be to facilitate work of the Legal Team through triage of tasks, performance of a diverse range of compliance and regulatory matters, conducting legal research, and provision of some administrative legal support.

You’ll be joining our legal team consisting of a General Counsel, Deputy General Counsel, two Senior Labour Legal Officers, Procurement Lead, and a Senior Legal Officer for Media and Comms. You will report to the Deputy General Counsel.

A successful Paralegal Officer will be interested in how charitable organizations law functions across global jurisdictions, has experience in legal research and providing support to attorneys, and will be responsive, organized, and supportive to enable the work of the Legal team.

If this sounds like you, we'd love for you to apply.

How you'll contribute

• Support the legal team by:

• Managing the triage of legal tasks and communication with requestors,

• Managing and coordinating the portfolio of service providers of the Legal team, and coordinating with the Procurement Lead new required procurement of the Legal team;

• Maintaining the overview of the Legal budget on behalf of the General Counsel;

• Reviewing, proofreading, preparing and formatting of legal documents;

• Facilitating compliance activities and processing routine legal data, ensuring follow-up on discrepancies to support the delivery of legal services;

• Conducting legal research and preparing summaries, guidelines and presentations based on research findings (topics are broad and include, but are not limited to, areas such as Intellectual Property, Data Protection, Digital Laws, Corporate Compliance, Media Law, and Human Rights);

• Performing other activities and providing support in any other ad hoc legal, regulatory and administrative matters.

Our ideal candidate has

• You hold a university degree in Law or equivalent and have 3-5 years of relevant paralegal or legal professional experience;

• You have successfully managed a high volume of information and documentation in a busy environment, previous compliance and legal research experience is an asset;

• You have an understanding or appetite to learn about large pieces of compliance legislations, notably for the EU the GDPR and the AI Act;

• You are organised and have a keen eye for detail;

• You have the ability to work in a team environment;

• You demonstrate a requirement for creative and analytical thinking to resolve issues within established frameworks;

• You act with integrity and discretion;

• You have strong networking skills that enable effective collaboration with a diverse group of stakeholders;

• Fluency in written and spoken English, our working language; additional languages are an asset.

• A genuine interest in and passion for addressing climate change.

• The disposition to work generously with colleagues in a high-trust global community of peers.

• The versatile and adaptable skills necessary to flourish in a fast-paced and wide-ranging work environment and work across time zones using tools such as Zoom, Slack and email (Google Workspace).

Why join us?

• Be part of a remote-first, international network of professionals working on climate, energy and nature and work with a bunch of dynamic, culturally diverse individuals who combine their passion to make a difference with a rigorous and results-oriented approach to work.

• 6 offline days (with no calls or meetings) per year to focus on your work

• Reduced working time on Fridays during the Dry Season (summer)

• Access to our employee assistance program to support your mental health and personal development

• Supplementary paid business closure for end-of-year holidays

• Maternity leave matched to 20 weeks for countries where regulation allows less than that

• Enjoy regular opportunities to connect and collabor

About Nebius:

Nebius is leading a new era in cloud infrastructure for the global AI economy. We are building a full-stack AI cloud platform that supports developers and enterprises from data and model training through to production deployment, without the cost and complexity of building large in-house AI/ML infrastructure.

Built by engineers, for engineers. From large-scale GPU orchestration to inference optimization, we own the hard problems across compute, storage, networking and applied AI.

Listed on Nasdaq (NBIS) and headquartered in Amsterdam, we have a global footprint with R&amp;D hubs across Europe, the UK, North America and Israel. Our team of 1,500+ includes hundreds of engineers with deep expertise across hardware, software and AI R&amp;D.

EAST COAST TIMEZONE ONLY&nbsp;

Nebius is building the infrastructure that powers the next generation of AI - and we're scaling fast. In line with our values, we're committed to an inclusive environment where everyone can thrive, and we're looking for an experienced Environmental, Health &amp; Safety Manager to set the direction, standards and capability that keep our expanding operations safe, compliant and sustainable. &nbsp;

This is no ordinary role. We need a decisive, credible leader who owns the regional EHS agenda - someone who can set strategy, build a high-performing team, and act as the trusted voice of EHS at leadership level, while staying close enough to the detail to guide, challenge and support when it matters most. &nbsp;

This is a genuine opportunity to shape how EHS operates as Nebius scales. You'll have real autonomy to set priorities, build capability across your team and sites, and influence decisions at a regional and business level - backed by strong support from senior stakeholders and a growing global EHS function. &nbsp;

You'll be accountable for the performance, maturity and culture of EHS across your region - leading through others, setting the standards your team and sites work to, and stepping in personally when situations demand calm, experienced judgement under pressure. Above all, you'll help make sound EHS practice a source of competitive advantage for Nebius, not a compliance exercise. &nbsp;

What you'll do &nbsp;

• Set and own the regional EHS plan , turning policy and legislation into clear priorities and controls for your sites.

• Translate the global EHS strategy into everyday practice across your region, making sure teams are well supported and equipped with the toolkit to advance site EHS performance.

• Lead, manage and develop local teams - setting objectives, building capability, and owning outcomes.

• Act as the senior EHS voice with regional and site leadership - advising on risk and holding the business to account.

• Own regional governance and assurance - audits, inspections, and the quality of your team's risk assessments and controls.

• Take personal command of major incidents and crises - leading investigations, building a learning culture, and reporting to executive level with composure under pressure.

• Set the standard for construction, commissioning,&nbsp; operations and other high-risk activity, equipping specialists to manage risk on the ground.

• Own the regional EHS performance narrative - turning data into insight that drives decisions and accountable action plans.

• Review EHS data and performance metrics regularly, acting on trends and insights to drive continuous improvement across the region.

• Support the wider environmental and sustainability agenda, partnering with teams to embed it across your region.

• Represent Nebius with regulators, industry bodies and partners, protecting its credibility on EHS.

• Champion Nebius's culture and values, embedding EHS as core to how the business operates. &nbsp;

